d URLs correspond correctly between the frontend and backend code.\n\n### Highest Priority Comment\n\nComment: There is a potential issue with the view_note() function, specifically in how it handles the deletion and editing of notes. The current implementation modifies the notes list in memory but does not ensure that the note is found before attempting to edit or delete it. If a note is not found, it could lead to an error when trying to access its attributes.\n\nSuggestion for Revision:\n1. Before attempting to delete or edit a note, check if the note exists. If it does not exist, return an appropriate error message or redirect the user back to the dashboard.\n\nRevised Code for view_note() Function:\npython\n@app.route(\'/view_note/&lt;note_id&gt;\', methods=[\'GET\', \'POST\'])\ndef view_note(note_id):\n    notes = read_notes()\n    note = next((note for note in notes if note[\'id\'] == note_id), None)\n    \n    if note is None:\n        return redirect(url_for(\'dashboard\'))  # Redirect if note not found\n\n    if request.method == \'POST\':\n        if \'delete_note_button\' in request.form:\n            notes.remove(note)\n            with open(NOTES_FILE, \'w\') as f:\n                for n in notes:\n                    f.write(f"{n[\'id\']}|{n[\'title\']}|{n[\'content\']}\\n")\n            return redirect(url_for(\'dashboard\'))\n        elif \'edit_note_button\' in request.form:\n            note[\'title\'] = request.form[\'note_title_field\']\n            note[\'content\'] = request.form[\'note_content_field\']\n            with open(NOTES_FILE, \'w\') as f:\n                for n in notes:\n                    f.write(f"{n[\'id\']}|{n[\'title\']}|{n[\'content\']}\\n")\n            return redirect(url_for(\'dashboard\'))\n    \n    return render_template(\'view_note.html\', note=note)\n\n\n### Instructions on Fix\n1. Update the view_note() function as shown above to include a check for the existence of the note.\n2. Ensure that the application handles